1️⃣ ISNULL — replace NULL with a fallback value
SQL Server → ISNULL | Oracle → NVL | MySQL → IFNULL Takes exactly 2 values. Fast.

ISNULL(value, replacement_value)
-- Replace missing shipping address with 'Unknown'
SELECT ISNULL(shipping_address, 'Unknown')
FROM sales.customers;

-- Fall back from shipping address to billing address
SELECT ISNULL(shipping_address, billing_address)
FROM sales.customers;
2️⃣ COALESCE — first non-NULL value from a list
ANSI standard — works in ALL databases (SQL Server, Oracle, MySQL, PostgreSQL...). Takes unlimited values. Slightly slower than ISNULL, but portable and flexible.

COALESCE(value1, value2, value3, ...)
-- returns the first value that is NOT NULL
SELECT COALESCE(shipping_address, billing_address, 'N/A')
FROM sales.customers;
ISNULL vs COALESCE
ISNULL	COALESCE
Values	Only 2	Unlimited
Speed	Faster	Slightly slower
Portability	SQL Server only	All databases
Equivalents	NVL (Oracle), IFNULL (MySQL)	ANSI standard
3️⃣ NULLIF — returns NULL when two values are equal
NULLIF(value1, value2)
-- if value1 = value2 → returns NULL
-- if value1 <> value2 → returns value1
-- Treat 'Unknown' as if it were NULL
SELECT NULLIF(shipping_address, 'Unknown');

-- Treat billing address as NULL when it matches shipping
SELECT NULLIF(shipping_address, billing_address);
⭐ Use case: avoid divide-by-zero
-- quantity = 0 would give a divide-by-zero error.
-- NULLIF turns 0 into NULL, and division by NULL → NULL (no error)
SELECT orderid,
       sales,
       quantity,
       sales / NULLIF(quantity, 0) AS price
FROM sales.orders;
4️⃣ IS NULL / IS NOT NULL — testing for NULL
⚠️ You can NEVER write value = NULL — the result is always unknown (never true). Use IS NULL (true if NULL) and IS NOT NULL (true if value exists).

-- Customers who have no scores
SELECT * FROM sales.customers WHERE score IS NULL;

-- Customers who have scores
SELECT * FROM sales.customers WHERE score IS NOT NULL;
5️⃣ Use Case: NULLs with aggregations
AVG(score) ignores NULL rows (averages only the rows that have a score). AVG(COALESCE(score, 0)) counts NULL rows as 0 — same number of rows in the denominator, so the result is lower.

SELECT customerid,
       score,
       AVG(score) OVER ()            AS avgscore,   -- NULLs skipped
       AVG(COALESCE(score, 0)) OVER () AS avgscore2 -- NULLs counted as 0
FROM sales.customers;
👉 Decide which behaviour you want: skip missing data or treat missing as zero.

6️⃣ Use Case: concatenation with NULLs
In SQL Server, anything + NULL = NULL. So FirstName + ' ' + LastName becomes NULL when LastName is missing.

SELECT customerid,
       FirstName,
       LastName,
       FirstName + ' ' + COALESCE(LastName, '') AS FullName, -- '' keeps the row non-NULL
       score,
       COALESCE(score, 0) + 10 AS ScoreWithBonus            -- NULL score treated as 0
FROM sales.customers;
7️⃣ Use Case: NULLs in joins
Join keys that can be NULL never match (NULL = NULL is unknown). Wrap both sides with ISNULL / COALESCE so NULL keys match NULL keys.

SELECT a.year, a.type, b.sales
FROM table1 AS a
JOIN table2 AS b
  ON a.year = b.year
 AND ISNULL(a.type, '') = ISNULL(b.type, '');   -- '=' not '==' in SQL
8️⃣ Use Case: NULLs in sorting
Order by score lowest → highest, with NULLs appearing last (SQL Server sorts NULLs first by default).

-- Default: NULLs come first (in SQL Server)
SELECT customerid, score
FROM Sales.customers
ORDER BY score;

-- Force NULLs to the end, two techniques:
SELECT customerid,
       score,
       COALESCE(score, 99999)                                -- technique 1: big number pushes NULLs last
       -- CASE WHEN score IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS flag  -- technique 2: flag column, ORDER BY flag, score
FROM Sales.customers
ORDER BY COALESCE(score, 99999);
9️⃣ Storage best practices — how to represent "missing"?
Option	Rule	Verdict
1	Use NULLs and empty strings '', but avoid blank spaces ' '	❌ Avoid — empty strings are ambiguous
2	Use only NULLs — avoid empty strings and blank spaces	✅ Recommended — one consistent way to say "missing"
3	Use only a default value like 'Unknown', avoid NULLs	⚠️ Works for display, but pollutes data and breaks aggregations/filters
Bottom line: store missing values as NULL; use ISNULL / COALESCE / NULLIF at the display layer to control how they appear.

